//
// PROBE: `io.textfile` and `io.textfile.` — a file's lines as a LIST.
//
// **The probe builds its own inputs**, which it could not have done before
// this month: `system.shell` runs `printf` to write each file, so the bytes
// are exact — a string literal cannot be trusted to carry `\r` or a trailing
// newline through two lexers, and `printf` can. Every path is absolute and
// under `/tmp`, because two harnesses run a probe from two working
// directories (F153), and the probe removes what it writes before and after.
//
// **F159: with a final newline, the last line comes back twice.** The second
// clause below is the first with one byte added, and it answers one element
// more: the last line again, *with its terminator attached*. That is nearly
// every text file. The CRLF clause shows the two copies come from different
// code — the first is stripped of `\r\n` cleanly, the duplicate is the raw
// tail. A *single* line does it differently again: its extra element is an
// empty string, because a line starting at offset zero never takes the
// reader's indexed path.
//
// Preserved, and by using the reference's own crate at its own version:
// reconstructing those offsets by hand would be describing the behaviour
// rather than having it. Blank lines are kept as empty strings, and leading
// and trailing spaces survive.
//
// **Three inputs are deliberately absent**, because each ends a program:
//
// - an **empty file** is the crate's own `Empty file` error;
// - a file that **begins with a line terminator** never returns in the
//   reference (F160), and Bund2 reports instead (D39, D97);
// - **invalid UTF-8 after two readable lines** aborts the reference with
//   `index out of bounds` (F161), and Bund2 reports the line instead (D37).
//
// The last two have no oracle answer to capture at all. All three are
// unit-tested in `crates/bund2-stdlib/src/host.rs`.
//
"/tmp/bund2-probe-textfile.txt" fs.rm drop

"probe: without a final newline, the lines are the lines" println
"printf 'one\ntwo\nthree' > /tmp/bund2-probe-textfile.txt" system.shell drop
"/tmp/bund2-probe-textfile.txt" io.textfile println

"probe: F159 — with one, the last line is answered twice and keeps its newline" println
"printf 'one\ntwo\nthree\n' > /tmp/bund2-probe-textfile.txt" system.shell drop
"/tmp/bund2-probe-textfile.txt" io.textfile println

"probe: a single line with a newline answers the line and an empty string" println
"printf 'solo\n' > /tmp/bund2-probe-textfile.txt" system.shell drop
"/tmp/bund2-probe-textfile.txt" io.textfile println

"probe: CRLF is stripped from the lines and kept on the duplicate" println
"printf 'one\r\ntwo\r\n' > /tmp/bund2-probe-textfile.txt" system.shell drop
"/tmp/bund2-probe-textfile.txt" io.textfile println

"probe: a blank line in the middle is kept as an empty string" println
"printf 'one\n\nthree' > /tmp/bund2-probe-textfile.txt" system.shell drop
"/tmp/bund2-probe-textfile.txt" io.textfile println

"probe: leading and trailing spaces survive" println
"printf '  padded  \nend' > /tmp/bund2-probe-textfile.txt" system.shell drop
"/tmp/bund2-probe-textfile.txt" io.textfile println

"probe: io.textfile. reads the workbench and answers there" println
"/tmp/bund2-probe-textfile.txt" . io.textfile.

"/tmp/bund2-probe-textfile.txt" fs.rm drop
